* START-DESCRIPTION:
*
*    call $netcred(action, server, record, pin, err)
*
* A LOGIN IS NOT A PASSWORD.  What is kept for one person on one server is the SCRAM-SHA-256 material derived from the password
* (RFC 5802 / 7677): the salt and the iteration count the server gave, the ClientKey and the ServerKey.  That is enough to log in
* to that one server as that one remote user; it is not enough to learn the password or to try it anywhere else.  THE PASSWORD IS
* NEVER STORED OR PASSED HERE, and nothing but those derived values is ever written.
*
* WHERE.  C:\ProgramData\SD\sd-net\<windows user>\<server name> on Windows (Linux: /var/lib/sd-net/<linux user>/<server name>).  The directory
* is made by set.netlogin as the person (so it is owned by the person and, by inheritance from sd-net, closed to everyone else but SYSTEM and
* Administrators) and sits OUTSIDE every account directory, so an account program whose OS-access gate is off cannot reach it with OPENSEQ.
* The user is the user the process runs as (kernel K$USERNAME).  An API session runs as a different identity with the user's name set on it,
* so the ownership test below fails there: this is for a local session of the person who made the login.
*
* ONE LINE, mode 600:   v1|remote user|salt|iterations|ClientKey|ServerKey|pin|created
*
*    salt, ClientKey and ServerKey are base64 as the SDEXT SCRAM calls give them; pin is the SHA-256 hash of the server's public key
*    that the definition held when the login was made (64 lower case hex digits); created is yyyy-mm-dd.
*
* TRUST.  EVERY USE of the file or of its directory is preceded by SD_FILE_PRIVATE (a C test: a plain file, not a link, owned by the
* running user, an ACL that names nobody but the running user, SYSTEM and Administrators, in a directory that passes the same test -
* what ssh's mode test is on Linux).  A file or directory that fails it is refused (err 12459) and nothing in it is used.
*
* HONEST LIMIT.  An account that holds the sh or os.execute gate can read anything its Linux user can read, this file included,
* exactly as it can read ~/.ssh.
*
*    action  READ    record is returned as a dynamic array, one value to an attribute:
*                       1 remote user  2 salt  3 iterations  4 ClientKey  5 ServerKey  6 pin  7 created
*                    pin must be the pin of the definition as it stands now (64 hex digits); a login made for a different pin is
*                    refused (err 12460) and record is null.
*            WRITE   record is given in the same layout.  Attributes 1 to 5 are required; attribute 6 is ignored and the pin
*                    ARGUMENT is what is stored; attribute 7 is the date or null for today.  The file is made with umask 077 under
*                    the name <server>.new, tested, and renamed over the old one, so there is never a half written login and an
*                    existing login is untouched by any failure.
*            DELETE  removes the file.
*            EXISTS  record is returned 1 when the login is there and trusted, 0 when it is not there (no error); a file that is
*                    there but not trusted is err 12459 and record 0.
*    server  lower case letter, then up to 15 lower case letters or digits (as !netdefs)
*    pin     READ and WRITE only
*
*    err is null or  message number : value mark : its arguments :
*       12457 server name not valid  12458 no login stored  12459 not trusted  12460 made for a different pin
*       12461 damaged or unreadable  12462 could not be stored or removed  12463 no login can be kept for this user
*       12464 the pin was not given  2018 unknown action
*
* END-DESCRIPTION
*
* START-CODE

$internal
subroutine netcred(action, server, record, pin, err)
$catalog $netcred

$include int$keys.h
$include keys.h

*  25 Oct 26 Windows port - WHERE.  Linux keeps the logins in /var/lib/sd-net (root:root 755), a directory for each person made by sd-elevate
*  netcred-dir.  Here the root is the directory sd-net beside the sdsys directory, under the SD data directory (C:\ProgramData\SD\sd-net),
*  made by the installer with an ACL that lets an SD user create a subdirectory but gives the group sdusers nothing inside the ones others made:
*  the creator owns the new directory and its ACL holds only that person, SYSTEM and Administrators (gplbld\secure-sdnet.ps1).  SD_FILE_PRIVATE
*  reads that ACL on every use, so a directory that is not what it should be is refused.  A test points a COPY of this program at a scratch directory.
$define NETCRED.DIRNAME 'sd-net'
$define NETCRED.UMASK 63                   ;* 077: the file is private from its first byte (the ACL, not the mode, is what protects it here)

*  Assigned first for BCOMP: an "is not assigned a value" warning fails the bootstrap.
   err = ''
   act = upcase(trim(action))
   lu = ''
   cred.dir = ''
   path = ''
   tmp = ''
   bad = ''
   line = ''
   one = ''
   f = 0
   tmp.f = 0
   made = @false
   old.umask = 0
   d = 0
   created = ''
   chk.text = ''
   chk.set = ''
   chk.ok = @true
   what = ''
   pin.ok = @false
   dir.there = @false
   n = 0
   i = 0
   k = 0
   root = ''
   marks = char(251):char(252):char(253):char(254):char(255)
   lc.set = 'abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz'
   digits = '0123456789'
   hex.set = digits:'abcdef'
   b64 = 'ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ':lc.set:digits:'+/='
   os.chars = 'ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ':lc.set:digits:'._-'

   if act # 'READ' and act # 'WRITE' and act # 'DELETE' and act # 'EXISTS' then
      err = 2018:@vm:action   ;* Unexpected token (xx)
      return
   end

*  ---- The server name, and whose login it is.
   chk.text = server ; chk.set = lc.set:digits ; gosub check.chars
   if len(server) < 1 or len(server) > 16 or not(chk.ok) or index(lc.set, server[1, 1], 1) = 0 then
      err = 12457:@vm:server
      return
   end

   lu = kernel(K$USERNAME, 0)
*  A Windows user name may hold a space; nothing that is a path separator, a drive or a wild card is let through (os.chars and the space).
   chk.text = lu ; chk.set = os.chars : ' ' ; gosub check.chars
   if lu = '' or lu = 'root' or lu = 'sdsys' or downcase(lu) = 'system' or len(lu) > MAX.USERNAME.LEN or not(chk.ok) or lu[1, 1] = '.' or lu[1, 1] = '-' or lu[1, 1] = ' ' or lu[len(lu), 1] = ' ' or lu[len(lu), 1] = '.' then
      err = 12463:@vm:lu
      return
   end

   root = convert('\', @ds, @sdsys)
   loop while root[len(root), 1] = @ds and len(root) > 3
      root = root[1, len(root) - 1]
   repeat
   root = root[1, index(root, @ds, count(root, @ds)) - 1] : @ds : NETCRED.DIRNAME
   cred.dir = root : @ds : lu
   path = cred.dir : @ds : server
   tmp = path : '.new'

   if act = 'EXISTS' then record = 0

*  ---- The directory: it must be there (WRITE needs it made by sd-elevate netcred-dir) and private, before anything is looked for in it.
   dir.there = ospath(cred.dir, OS$EXISTS)
   if not(dir.there) and act # 'WRITE' then
      if act = 'EXISTS' then return   ;* no directory, so no login: record is 0 and that is not an error
      err = 12458:@vm:server
      return
   end
   if not(dir.there) or SDEXT(cred.dir : @FM : 'DIR', @TRUE, SD_FILE_PRIVATE) # '1' then
      err = 12459:@vm:server:@vm:cred.dir
      return
   end

   begin case
      case act = 'EXISTS'
         if ospath(path, OS$EXISTS) then
            if SDEXT(path : @FM : 'FILE', @TRUE, SD_FILE_PRIVATE) = '1' then
               record = 1
            end else
               err = 12459:@vm:server:@vm:path
            end
         end

      case act = 'READ'
         record = ''
         gosub check.pin
         if not(pin.ok) then
            err = 12464
            return
         end
         if not(ospath(path, OS$EXISTS)) then
            err = 12458:@vm:server
            return
         end
         if SDEXT(path : @FM : 'FILE', @TRUE, SD_FILE_PRIVATE) # '1' then
            err = 12459:@vm:server:@vm:path
            return
         end
         line = ''
         openseq path to f else
            err = 12461:@vm:server:@vm:status()
            return
         end
         readseq line from f else line = ''
         closeseq f

*        A mark byte would make a second attribute out of one value: a line that holds one is damaged.
         if len(convert(marks, '', line)) # len(line) then
            err = 12461:@vm:server:@vm:'marks'
            return
         end
         convert '|' to @fm in line
         if dcount(line, @fm) # 8 or line<1> # 'v1' then
            err = 12461:@vm:server:@vm:'layout'
            return
         end
         for i = 1 to 7
            one<i> = line<i + 1>
         next i
         gosub check.record
         if bad # '' then
            err = 12461:@vm:server:@vm:bad
            return
         end
         if one<6> # pin then
            err = 12460:@vm:server
            return
         end
         record = one

      case act = 'WRITE'
         gosub check.pin
         if not(pin.ok) then
            err = 12464
            return
         end
         one = record
         one<6> = pin
         if one<7> = '' then
            d = date()
            one<7> = oconv(d, 'D4Y'):'-':fmt(oconv(d, 'DM'), '2"0"R'):'-':fmt(oconv(d, 'DD'), '2"0"R')
         end
         gosub check.record
         if bad # '' then
            err = 12461:@vm:server:@vm:bad
            return
         end

         line = 'v1'
         for i = 1 to 7
            line := '|' : one<i>
         next i

         if ospath(tmp, OS$EXISTS) then
            if not(ospath(tmp, OS$DELETE)) then
               err = 12462:@vm:server:@vm:tmp:@vm:status()
               return
            end
         end

*        Mode 600 from the first byte: the umask is 077 while the file is made, and is put back whatever happens.
         old.umask = umask(-1)
         void umask(NETCRED.UMASK)
         openseq tmp to tmp.f then
            weofseq tmp.f
            made = @true
         end else
            made = @true
            create tmp.f else made = @false
         end
         void umask(old.umask)
         if not(made) then
            err = 12462:@vm:server:@vm:tmp:@vm:status()
            return
         end

         writeseq line to tmp.f else
            err = 12462:@vm:server:@vm:tmp:@vm:status()
         end
         closeseq tmp.f
         line = ''
         one = ''

         if err = '' then
*           The new file is tested before it can replace anything: a file that did not come out private is not kept.
            if SDEXT(tmp : @FM : 'FILE', @TRUE, SD_FILE_PRIVATE) # '1' then
               err = 12459:@vm:server:@vm:tmp
            end
         end
         if err = '' then
            if not(osrename(tmp, path)) then
               err = 12462:@vm:server:@vm:path:@vm:status()
            end
         end
         if err # '' then void ospath(tmp, OS$DELETE)

      case act = 'DELETE'
         if not(ospath(path, OS$EXISTS)) then
            err = 12458:@vm:server
            return
         end
*        Only a plain private file is removed: a directory or a link with this name is not ours to remove with a recursive delete.
         if SDEXT(path : @FM : 'FILE', @TRUE, SD_FILE_PRIVATE) # '1' then
            err = 12459:@vm:server:@vm:path
            return
         end
         if not(ospath(path, OS$DELETE)) then
            err = 12462:@vm:server:@vm:path:@vm:status()
         end
   end case

   return

* ======================================================================
* check.chars - is every character of chk.text in chk.set?  Sets chk.ok.
check.chars:
   chk.ok = @true
   for k = 1 to len(chk.text)
      if index(chk.set, chk.text[k, 1], 1) = 0 then chk.ok = @false
   next k
   return

* ======================================================================
* check.record - are the seven values of "one" the right shape?  Sets bad to the name of the first one that is not, or null.  A login
* that was written by hand, damaged, or made for another purpose is not used: every value has the shape this program writes.
check.record:
   bad = ''
   what = one<1>
   chk.text = what ; chk.set = os.chars ; gosub check.chars
   if what = '' or len(what) > MAX.USERNAME.LEN or not(chk.ok) then bad = 'remote user'
   for i = 2 to 5
      if i = 3 then
         what = one<3>
         if len(what) < 1 or len(what) > 8 or not(what matches '1N0N') then
            bad = 'iterations'
         end else
            if what + 0 < SCRAM$MIN.ITER or what + 0 > SCRAM$MAX.ITER then bad = 'iterations'
         end
      end else
         what = one<i>
         chk.text = what ; chk.set = b64 ; gosub check.chars
         if len(what) < 4 or len(what) > 128 or not(chk.ok) then
            if i = 2 then bad = 'salt'
            if i = 4 then bad = 'ClientKey'
            if i = 5 then bad = 'ServerKey'
         end
      end
   next i
   what = one<6>
   chk.text = what ; chk.set = hex.set ; gosub check.chars
   if len(what) # 64 or not(chk.ok) then bad = 'pin'
   what = one<7>
   if not(what matches '4N"-"2N"-"2N') then bad = 'created'
   return

* ======================================================================
* check.pin - is the pin argument the 64 lower case hexadecimal digits of a pin?  Sets pin.ok.
check.pin:
   chk.text = pin ; chk.set = hex.set ; gosub check.chars
   pin.ok = (len(pin) = 64 and chk.ok)
   return

end

* END-CODE
